## Setup

You need a TeX distribution with `latexmk` (MacTeX)[% if use_python %], plus [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)[% endif %].

```sh
[% if use_python %]
uv sync      # creates .venv/ in the repo from pyproject.toml + uv.lock
[% endif %]
make         # builds every homework and set of lecture notes
```

Run every command from the repo root.

This repo was generated from a [Copier](https://copier.readthedocs.io) template (answers in
`.copier-answers.yml`). To pull in later template improvements, commit first, then run
`uvx --python 3.12 copier update`.

## Layout

```
tex/                  shared preamble (course.sty) + templates for new documents
scripts/course.sh     scaffolding logic behind `make new-hw` / `new-lec` / `slides`
course/               instructor material (read-only)
  slides/             lecNN-<topic>.pdf, lecNN-<topic>-annotated.pdf
  readings/
homework/hwN/
  hwN.tex             my solutions          -> build/hwN.pdf  (submitted to Gradescope)
  figures/  code/     my figures; my working copy of starter code + its data
  handout/            course-provided files (read-only): [[ hw_pdf_pattern | replace('{N}', 'N') ]], main.tex, ...
notes/NN-<topic>/
  lecNN.tex           my lecture notes      -> build/lecNN.pdf
  figures/
exams/  projects/
```

**Rule of thumb:** anything the course gives you goes in `course/` or in the assignment's
`handout/`, and nothing there is ever edited. Everything else is yours.

## Day-to-day workflows

### Starting a homework

```sh
make new-hw N=3 DUE="Mon Oct 19, 11:59 PM PT"
```

This creates `homework/hw3/` containing `hw3.tex` (homework number and due date already
filled in), plus `figures/`, `code/` and `handout/`. Then:

1. Put the course's files into `handout/`. Rename the assignment PDF to
   `[[ hw_pdf_pattern | replace('{N}', '3') ]]`, **not** `hw3.pdf` (see [Gotchas](#gotchas)).
2. Copy starter code **and its data files** into `code/`. `handout/` keeps the untouched
   originals so you can diff against them.
3. Transcribe each problem verbatim into a `problem` box followed by a `solution` block, and end
   each problem with `\clearpage` (one problem per page for Gradescope matching). Copy figures into
   `figures/` with clean names, or redraw small graphs in TikZ (see
   [Belief networks](#belief-networks)).
4. `make watch-hw3` while writing. Submit `homework/hw3/build/hw3.pdf`.

### After a lecture

1. Download the slides into `course/slides/` with their original names (`[[ slide_prefix ]] 06.pdf`, and/or
   the annotated version with `[[ annotated_marker ]]` in its name). The expected names are set in
   `scripts/course.conf`.
2. Create the notes:

   ```sh
   make new-lec TOPIC="Junction Trees"                    # next number, today's date
   make new-lec TOPIC="Junction Trees" N=6 DATE="Oct 13, 2026"
   make new-lec TOPIC="Junction Trees" DATE=""            # no date in the header
   ```

   This creates `notes/06-junction-trees/lec06.tex` and then files the slides as
   `course/slides/lec06-junction-trees.pdf` / `lec06-junction-trees-annotated.pdf`.
3. If the slides show up after the notes folder already exists, run `make slides`.
   It renames every `Lecture NN*.pdf` it can match to a `notes/NN-*/` folder and never
   overwrites existing files.

### Building

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `make` | build everything |
| `make hw2`, `make lec03` | build one document → `<its dir>/build/<name>.pdf` |
| `make homework`, `make notes` | build all of one kind |
| `make watch-hw2` | rebuild on every save (Ctrl+C to stop) |
| `make clean-hw2`, `make clean` | remove aux files, keep PDFs and SyncTeX |
| `make distclean` | delete every `build/` folder |

All output (PDF, `.synctex.gz`, aux files) goes to the document's `build/` folder, which is
gitignored. To call latexmk yourself, run it from the document's folder and **always** pass
`-outdir=build`. Otherwise aux files spill into the source folder.

New documents are picked up automatically: the Makefile globs `homework/hw*/hw*.tex` and
`notes/NN-*/lecNN.tex`, so there's nothing to register.

### VS Code

`.vscode/` configures LaTeX Workshop to build on save into `build/`, open the PDF in a tab, and
skip auto-cleaning so incremental rebuilds stay fast. SyncTeX works in both directions:
Cmd+click in the PDF jumps to the source.

Tasks (Cmd+Shift+B): **LaTeX: Compile**, **LaTeX: Clean**, **LaTeX: Compile + Clean**.
[% if use_python %]

The Python interpreter is set to `.venv/bin/python`. Terminal auto-activation is turned off on
purpose, so use `uv run` in terminals.

### Python

The environment lives in the in-repo `.venv/` (gitignored) and is managed by uv. There is no
direnv and nothing to activate.

```sh
uv add numpy scipy matplotlib              # add dependencies (updates pyproject.toml + uv.lock)
uv run python homework/hw1/code/main.py   # run inside the env
```

Code goes in `homework/hwN/code/` or `projects/<name>/`. Fix random seeds, and save figures as
PDF into the write-up's `figures/` so `\includegraphics{name}` picks them up directly.
[% endif %]

## LaTeX reference (`course.sty`)

Every document lives exactly two levels below the root and starts with:

```latex
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\makeatletter\def\input@path{{../../tex/}}\makeatother  % find tex/course.sty
\usepackage{course}
```

Course and student info (`\coursecode`, `\coursetitle`, `\coursename`, `\courseterm`,
`\instructor`, `\studentname`, `\studentpid`, `\studentemail`) is defined once at the top of
`course.sty`, and the templates' headers use it. Change something there and every document
picks it up on the next build.

### Homework

```latex
\begin{problem}{3.1}{Inference in a polytree}{6}   % number, title, points
  ...statement, verbatim from the handout...
  \begin{enumerate} \item ... \end{enumerate}       % labelled (a), (b), ...
\end{problem}

\begin{solution}
  \part{a} ...                                      % run-in "(a)" heading
  \part*{$(1) \iff (2)$} ...                        % heading without parentheses
\end{solution}
\clearpage
```

### Lecture notes

| Environment | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `definition`, `theorem`, `lemma`, `proposition`, `corollary`, `claim`, `example`, `remark` | numbered per section (Definition 2.1, ...); `\begin{theorem}[Name]` |
| `keyidea` | green box: takeaways (`[Title]` optional) |
| `question` | orange box: things to ask or follow up on |
| `aside` | gray box: rigor or connections beyond the lecture (`[Title]` optional) |
| `\slide{12}` | gray "[slide 12]" pointer to the instructor's slides |

Cross-reference with `\label{def:x}` + `\cref{def:x}`.

### Math macros

| Macro | Renders | Macro | Renders |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `\P(X \given Y)` | P(X \| Y) | `\KL{p}{q}` | D_KL(p ‖ q) |
| `\E`, `\Var`, `\Cov` | 𝔼, Var, Cov | `\ind{x > 0}` | 𝟙[x > 0] |
| `X \indep Y \given Z` | X ⫫ Y \| Z | `\nindep` | not independent |
| `\pa(X)` | pa(X) | `X_i \iid \Normal(0,1)` | iid ~ 𝒩 |
| `\argmax`, `\argmin` | with limits underneath | `\R`, `\N`, `\eqdef` | ℝ, ℕ, ≔ |

### Belief networks

```latex
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[bnnode] (X) {$X$};
  \node[bnnode, below left=1cm and 0.6cm of X] (Y) {$Y$};
  \draw[bnedge] (X) -- (Y);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

`bnnode` is filled with `probbg`, the problem-box background, so graphs drawn inside a problem
blend in. The `bayesnet` library (`\node[latent]`, `\node[obs]`, `\edge`) is loaded too.

### Code

Use `\begin{lstlisting} ... \end{lstlisting}`. Python is the default language.

### Adding macros

If a macro would be useful in more than one document, add it to `tex/course.sty`. Don't add it
to an individual `.tex` file. Also list it in `AGENTS.md`.

## How the machinery works

- **`Makefile`**: a thin wrapper. Build targets run
  `latexmk -pdf -synctex=1 -interaction=nonstopmode -file-line-error -outdir=build` inside the
  document's folder. latexmk tracks dependencies itself, so targets always run and latexmk
  decides whether anything needs rebuilding. Scaffolding targets call `scripts/course.sh`.
- **`scripts/course.sh`**:
  - `new-hw` / `new-lec` copy a template from `tex/` and strip its "how to use this template"
    header. They then fill fields by rewriting `\newcommand{\hwnum}{...}`, `\duedate`,
    `\lecnum`, `\lectopic` and `\lecdate` with `sed`, escaping LaTeX special characters in the
    values. Neither one overwrites an existing document.
  - `new-lec` numbers new notes one past the highest `notes/NN-*`, and slugifies the topic for the
    folder name.
  - `slides` takes the topic from the matching `notes/NN-<topic>/` folder.
  - Course-specific names (slide prefix, annotated marker, official PDF pattern) live in
    `scripts/course.conf`, which `course.sh` sources.
- **Templates** (`tex/hw_template.tex`, `tex/lecture_template.tex`): edit these to change what
  *future* documents look like. Existing documents aren't touched. Keep the
  `\newcommand{\field}{...}` lines on single lines, because the script matches them by pattern.

## Gotchas

- **Never name anything `hwN.pdf` except the build output.** A build would overwrite an
  official handout with that name. Official PDFs are `[[ hw_pdf_pattern | replace('{N}', 'N') ]]`.
- **The filesystem is case-insensitive** (`Homework` == `homework`). Do case-only renames via a
  temporary name.
- The repo holds solutions and instructor material, so any git remote must be **private**.
